\name{ComplexHeatmapAnnotationLayerProcessor}
\alias{ComplexHeatmapAnnotationLayerProcessor}
\title{ComplexHeatmap Annotation Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads one simple annotation track of a ComplexHeatmap heat map
(\code{\link[=complexheatmap_annotation_tracks]{complexheatmap_annotation_tracks()}}) as a layer of the heat map's
subplot, beside its \code{heat} layer, named by the annotation (\code{name}).

\strong{Shape.} Every track is read as a row of its own, one cell per column
(a column annotation) or per row (a row annotation) of the heat layer,
in the order the heat layer reads them: clustered, reordered and split
as drawn, a row track top first. Its \code{x} labels are the heat layer's
column or row labels, so moving between the heat layer and a column
track keeps the column.
\itemize{
\item A track mapped to a continuous colour scale is a 1-row \code{heat} layer:
\code{y} is the track's name, \code{points} its values, exactly; a missing value
is a hole (\code{null}).
\item A track mapped to discrete colours (characters, factors, logicals, or
numbers given discrete colours) is a \code{step} layer of one series, one
point per cell: \code{x} its label, \code{y} the level's place among the colour
mapping's levels (1, 2, ...), and \code{label} the level, which maidr.js
announces in place of \code{y}. A missing value is \code{y: null} without a
\code{label}. maidr.js's \code{heat} cell is a number only, so a level can be
announced only by a trace that carries a name beside its number.
}

\strong{Selectors.} Each cell is the rect it is drawn as
(\code{\link[=complexheatmap_name_annotations]{complexheatmap_name_annotations()}}): for \code{heat}, a one-row grid; for
\code{step}, one selector matching every cell in drawn order. A track whose
cells cannot be told apart is read without highlighting.
}
